---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import ThoughtProcess from '../../components/ThoughtProcess.astro';
import type { Metric } from '../../data/projects';

const repo = 'https://github.com/omkar-k0/werkbank';
const report = `${repo}/blob/main/reports/eval.md`;

const metrics: Metric[] = [
  { value: '95.8%', label: 'accuracy on the 40% of hand-written requests it routed on its own', evidence: 'measured', source: 'reports/eval.md, hand-written set n = 60' },
  { value: '23.3%', label: 'of hand-written requests sent to the review queue instead of guessed', evidence: 'measured', source: 'reports/eval.md' },
  { value: '96.8%', label: 'top-1 accuracy after a new shop corrects 100 orders (from 75.5%)', evidence: 'synthetic', source: 'reports/eval.md, feedback loop on synthetic orders' },
  { value: '—', label: 'accuracy on real shop orders: no shop has used it yet', evidence: 'pending', source: 'needs a live shop' },
];

const facts = [
  { label: 'Type', value: 'Product' },
  { label: 'When', value: 'October 2026' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Sole developer: classifier, gate, app, auth, eval' },
  { label: 'Origin', value: 'A real client brief from a furniture shop with three workshops' },
  { label: 'Status', value: 'Live demo · EN and DE · 52 tests' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'plain', label: 'In plain language' },
  { id: 'question', label: 'The question' },
  { id: 'obvious', label: 'Why the obvious fails' },
  { id: 'gate', label: 'The gate' },
  { id: 'system', label: 'Thought process' },
  { id: 'decisions', label: 'Decisions' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'What came out' },
  { id: 'refuses', label: 'What it refuses to claim' },
  { id: 'broke', label: 'What broke' },
  { id: 'open', label: 'Still open' },
];
---
<CaseStudy
  title="Werkbank"
  question="Which workshop should take this furniture order?"
  description="Order routing for furniture shops with their own workshops: cheap layers first, a calibrated confidence gate, a review queue for unsure orders, and a model that learns from every correction."
  crumb="Product · 03"
  links={[
    { label: 'Open the demo shop', href: 'https://werkbank-gamma.vercel.app', primary: true },
    { label: 'Source on GitHub', href: repo },
    { label: 'Evaluation report', href: report },
  ]}
  metrics={metrics}
  facts={facts}
  toc={toc}
  stack={['Next.js', 'TypeScript', 'Postgres', 'Naive Bayes', 'Vitest', 'PWA', 'Google sign-in (OIDC)', 'Vercel']}
  prev={{ href: '/work/faultline/', label: 'faultline' }}
  next={{ href: '/#finbert-fusion', label: 'FinBERT fusion' }}
>
  <section id="plain">
    <h2><span class="num">01</span>In plain language</h2>
    <p>
      A furniture shop with its own workshops gets requests like "sofa cushion torn, needs new foam" or "Tisch
      wackelt, Bein lose". Someone has to decide which bench each job goes to: carpentry, upholstery, polish and
      paint, or metalwork.
    </p>
    <p>
      Werkbank reads the request the way the owner would write it on a job slip and sends it to the right
      workshop. It also labels the job type, urgency and effort. When it is not sure, it does not guess: the
      order waits in a review queue. Every correction the owner makes teaches that shop's model.
    </p>
    <p>
      It started as a real brief from a furniture shop with three in-house workshops. I turned it into a
      product any shop can sign up for, in English and German.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="question">
    <h2><span class="num">02</span>The question</h2>
    <p class="callout">
      How many orders can go straight to the right workshop without a person, and can the system tell which
      ones those are?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="obvious">
    <h2><span class="num">03</span>Why the obvious approach fails</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Keyword rules</strong> are easy to explain to a shop owner, but customers do not use the shop's
      words. "Recliner mechanism stuck" is a metalwork job with no metal word in it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A classifier that always answers</strong> sends every unclear order somewhere. A wrong routing is
      worse than a slow one: the job sits at the wrong bench until someone notices.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>An LLM on every order</strong> costs money per order, needs an API key in every shop, and still
      answers confidently when the request is vague.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="gate">
    <h2><span class="num">04</span>The gate</h2>
    <p>
      Two cheap layers run in under a millisecond: a keyword lexicon in English and German, and a naive Bayes
      model trained on seed data plus the shop's own corrections. Their blended score goes through a gate.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Condition (balanced setting)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Auto-route</td><td class="n">confidence ≥ 0.85 and margin ≥ 0.5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Route, flagged "check"</td><td class="n">confidence ≥ 0.55</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Review queue</td><td>Lower confidence, almost no known words, the two layers strongly disagree, or the shop has no workshop for the predicted job</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Naive Bayes is known to be over-confident, so its scores are tempered by a temperature fitted on held-out
      phrasings (2.5). The interface never shows more than "99%+". Shops can move the gate between strict,
      balanced and relaxed.
    </p>
    <p>
      An optional language model only sees orders that are already in review. It must quote the request word
      for word, or its answer is thrown away. An LLM decision is never marked as automatic.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="system">
    <h2><span class="num">05</span>Thought process</h2>
    <p>Left to right, the path one order takes, from the owner's typing to the shop's next model.</p>
    <ThoughtProcess
      steps={[
        { title: 'Read', points: ['Free text, English or German', 'Live preview while typing', 'Due date taken into account'], takeaway: 'The owner writes the way they talk.' },
        { title: 'Score', points: ['Keyword lexicon, EN + DE', 'Naive Bayes: seed + corrections', 'Temperature 2.5 calibration'], takeaway: 'Two cheap opinions, in under a millisecond.' },
        { title: 'Gate', points: ['Auto ≥ 0.85, margin ≥ 0.5', 'Check ≥ 0.55', 'Disagreement → review'], takeaway: 'Sure, fairly sure, or ask.' },
        { title: 'Review', points: ['Best guess shown to the owner', 'Optional LLM, quote required', 'Never marked automatic'], takeaway: 'Unsure orders wait for a person.', model: true },
        { title: 'Learn', points: ['Every reassignment adds counts', 'No training job needed', "Per-shop model"], takeaway: 'Corrections make tomorrow easier.' },
      ]}
      note="The model predicts four job types, not workshop names. Each shop maps its own workshops onto them, so one model serves every shop."
    />
  </section>

  <section id="decisions">
    <h2><span class="num">06</span>Decisions, and what they cost</h2>
    <div class="decisions">
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Naive Bayes</strong>
          <p>One correction just adds counts. The shop's model updates instantly, with no training job.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>A model that needs retraining</strong>
          <p>Every correction would wait for a training run before the shop saw any benefit.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Fix the gate before testing</strong>
          <p>Thresholds were set before looking at test results.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Search for the best thresholds</strong>
          <p>The search on validation data collapsed to the minimum threshold, so it was dropped and the thresholds were fixed by hand.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Rules for urgency and effort</strong>
          <p>They depend on a few explicit words and the due date. "No rush" is checked before "rush".</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>A third model</strong>
          <p>A shop owner can read a rule and see why a job was marked urgent.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Test on words it never trained on</strong>
          <p>The synthetic test set uses sentence templates and synonyms that never appear in training, plus 60 messy requests written by hand.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>A random split of the generated data</strong>
          <p>That would test whether it remembers its own templates.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="results">
    <h2><span class="num">07</span>What came out</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Test set</th><th>Auto-routed</th><th>Auto accuracy</th><th>To review</th><th>Accuracy of all routed</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Hand-written, messy (n = 60)</td><td class="n">40.0%</td><td class="n">95.8%</td><td class="n">23.3%</td><td class="n">93.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Synthetic, unseen phrasing (n = 800)</td><td class="n">18.9%</td><td class="n">95.4%</td><td class="n">36.1%</td><td class="n">84.0%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The gate holds: whatever skips review is right about 95% of the time on both sets. What changes is how
      much it dares to skip. On phrasing it has never seen, it sends more to review instead of guessing.
    </p>
    <h3>Strict or relaxed: the trade-off a shop chooses</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Auto threshold</th><th>Auto-routed</th><th>Auto accuracy</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="n">0.50</td><td class="n">71.8%</td><td class="n">82.8%</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="n">0.70</td><td class="n">43.6%</td><td class="n">86.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="n">0.85 (default)</td><td class="n">18.9%</td><td class="n">95.4%</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="n">0.95</td><td class="n">4.4%</td><td class="n">100.0%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3>The feedback loop</h3>
    <p>
      A simulated shop that phrases things in ways the seed model never saw corrects its first orders. Then the
      model is measured on 400 later orders:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Corrections</th><th>Top-1 accuracy</th><th>Auto-routed</th><th>Review queue</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="n">0</td><td class="n">75.5%</td><td class="n">20.8%</td><td class="n">34.8%</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="n">50</td><td class="n">92.0%</td><td class="n">63.7%</td><td class="n">12.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="n">100</td><td class="n">96.8%</td><td class="n">82.5%</td><td class="n">6.3%</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="n">200</td><td class="n">98.5%</td><td class="n">87.5%</td><td class="n">4.0%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="caption">All from <a href={report}>reports/eval.md</a>, generated 26 Oct 2026. Seed model trained on 2,400 synthetic requests.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="refuses">
    <h2><span class="num">08</span>What it refuses to claim</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Real-world accuracy.</strong> There is no public dataset of furniture-shop tickets, so training data
      is synthetic, and synthetic test data is easier than real orders. The true number is unknown until a shop
      uses it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The 400-correction result.</strong> It reaches 100%, but by then the test orders are close to what
      it trained on. The evaluation report says not to quote it, so it is not quoted here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="broke">
    <h2><span class="num">09</span>What broke</h2>
    <h3>Three hand-written orders went to the wrong bench</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Request</th><th>Right</th><th>Routed to</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>"need a study table built into the corner, 120x60, white laminate top"</td><td>Carpentry</td><td>Finishing (auto, 0.93)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"old teak chest, grandma's, wants it restored. finish is black and sticky"</td><td>Finishing</td><td>Carpentry (check, 0.74)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"recliner mechanism stuck, footrest won't come out"</td><td>Metalwork</td><td>Upholstery (check, 0.66)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Only the first one skipped review entirely. "White laminate top" pulled it towards finishing. The other two
      were routed but flagged for the owner to check.
    </p>
    <h3>German is the weaker language</h3>
    <p>
      The keyword lexicon is mostly English. In a small manual check, 3 of 5 German example sentences went to the
      review queue. They were not routed wrongly, but the shop gets less automation in German.
    </p>
    <h3>Job type is mediocre</h3>
    <p>
      Repair, custom, refurbish or assembly is right only 70% of the time on the synthetic set (75% hand-written).
      It is shown as a label, not used for routing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="open">
    <h2><span class="num">10</span>Still open</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A real shop using it, and accuracy measured on real orders.</li>
      <li>A bigger German lexicon.</li>
      <li>Login rate limiting, and ending open sessions when a password is reset.</li>
      <li>Payments: picking a plan records it, but billing is not connected.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
